o has worked in the British  media now for the best part of a decade  Boris Johnson is treated as a bit of a  circus a bit of a joke a bit of a laugh  but he is somebody who was peddled  racism  he's serially dishonest he's a charlatan  but we're not having that conversation  because again and it's worth emphasizing  this if you are from a posh background  you can more or less get away with  anything in this country as a reward you  think he has been apart from this  interview he's done this morning he has  been notably absent from this campaign  hasn't he he has and I think that's  deliberate policy because the biggest  obstacle to Boris becoming Prime  Minister is Boris himself Boris says  things that perhaps he shouldn't say  sets the cat amongst the pigeons so I  think there's been a deliberate strategy  of let's keep Boris out of the news he's  lost lots of weight he's got a haircut  he's looking more serious he's given  this interview to my paper my colleague  Tim Shipman in the Sunday Times today  which in which we've learned he says I'm  going to go I'm not going to give the  thirty nine billion and let me get a  deal I'm prepared to walk away etc etc  but I do think going forward and if we  get you know the television debates  we've learnt what happened  time when the conservative leadership  you know the process was curtailed when  Andrea led some dropped out and we had  Theresa May appointed by default so to  speak and that didn't work out very well  I mean she wasn't tested she was a  terrible campaign and look where that  has got the Conservative Party so I  think it is really important that Boris  should participate in the TV debates and  when they happen I don't think he'll be  that keen but I think we need to see him  if he might well be prime minister of  this country yeah I mean with all due  respect I think that contribution itself  reveals part of the problem we're more  likely in the British media to hear  people discussing him losing weight and  getting a haircut than the fact that he  once conspired with a criminal on the  phone to talk about beating up a  journalist all for example being sacked  for dishonesty including by The Times  newspaper you'll see your sister paper  for dishonesty the fact that he's  somebody he was peddled bigotry and  racism once spoke of course not that  long ago about jokes about clearing the  bodies from Libya to turn it into a  tourist resort this is somebody he was  regarded as an embarrassment across  Europe he reduced this country to the I  mean the many things you can say about  Teresa may and I have said them but one  of the one of the many abject things she  did is appoint this clown and a  dangerous clown as foreign secretary and  I just think all too often the  superficial coverage you can if you you  know a guy he's posh and and and speaks  a bit a Latin and is swallowed a  thesaurus and ruffles your hair  occasionally like this you can more or  less get away with anything and then  half the political lobby go get the  knees it's absurd  oh and Catherine I'm afraid we're gonna  have to leave it there but thank you  both for coming on this afternoon thank  you

